ment having CONFIRMED status preventing cancellation, not about the order already existing. This is semantically wrong.\n\nHowever, I notice that the test is checking for the error envelope format, not the specific code. Let me re-read the requirement to see if it specifies which error code to use.\n\nThe requirement says:\n\"If any shipment of the order is CONFIRMED, the cancel is rejected with HTTP 409 **and nothing changes**: the order stays ACTIVE and every shipment keeps the status it had.\"\n\nIt doesn't specify the error code, just the HTTP status. And the test only checks that the status is 409 and nothing changes, not the specific error code.\n\nActually, looking at my test more carefully:\n```kotlin\n@Test\nfun `cancel fails if order has a CONFIRMED shipment`() {\n    // ...\n    mockMvc.perform(post(\"/orders/O-9/cancel\"))\n        .andExpect(status().isConflict)\n        .andExpect(jsonPath(\"$.error.code\").value(\"ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS\"))\n```\n\nI did assert the error code is ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS, which is wrong semantically. Let me think about this more carefully. The requirement doesn't specify which error code, so I have some freedom. But ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS is clearly meant for the create endpoint case, not for the cancel endpoint case.\n\nLooking at the requirement again:\n\"If any shipment of the order is CONFIRMED, the cancel is rejected with HTTP 409\"\n\nThis is a state conflict (the order is in a state where it cannot be cancelled), not a duplicate-key conflict. So using ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS is semantically incorrect.\n\nHowever, the requirement also says:\n\"5. A repeated cancel succeeds; a cancel blocked by a `CONFIRMED` shipment changes nothing; an unknown order is refused; a shipment for a cancelled order is refused.\"\n\nThis just lists the cases but doesn't specify error codes. And the note says \"Do not add new Maven dependency\" and \"Do not modify files outside the order and shipment features and the shared `api` package, except tests if required.\"\n\nI can add error codes to the api package, which is allowed. So I should add a new error code for this case.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the design. The requirement says the cancellation is \"rejected with HTTP 409\". The current implementation throws ConflictException, which returns HTTP 409. The question is: what error code should we use?\n\nLooking at my implementation again, I see I used ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS, which is wrong. But since the test doesn't fail, and the requirement doesn't specify the error code, I could:\n\n1. Leave it as is (but semantically wrong)\n2. Add a new error code like ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT (better semantics)\n3. Use a different existing error code\n\nActually, I realize I made an error. Let me check my implementation again by looking at what I wrote in OrderController.\n\nLooking back at the IMPLEMENTATION phase, I see:\n```kotlin\nif (shipments.any { it.status == ShipmentStatus.CONFIRMED }) {\n    throw ConflictException(\n        ErrorCode.ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS,\n        \"Cannot cancel order with confirmed shipments\",\n    )\n}\n```\n\nSo yes, I used ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS, which is semantically wrong. The error code ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS means \"you tried to create something with an ID that already exists\", not \"the current state prevents this operation\".\n\nBut the tests passed, which means the tests don't check the specific error code for this case. Looking back at my test:\n\n```kotlin\nmockMvc.perform(post(\"/orders/O-9/cancel\"))\n    .andExpect(status().isConflict)\n    .andExpect(jsonPath(\"$.error.code\").value(\"ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS\"))\n```\n\nI did assert ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS, which means I'm testing for the wrong error code!\n\nWait, let me re-read the requirement to see if it specifies what error code to use for this case. The acceptance criteria says:\n\"5. A repeated cancel succeeds; a cancel blocked by a `CONFIRMED` shipment changes nothing; an unknown order is refused; a shipment 