D - resource not found\n- ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS - duplicate creation\n- SHIPMENT_NOT_FOUND - resource not found\n- SHIPMENT_ALREADY_EXISTS - duplicate creation\n- VALIDATION_FAILED - input validation\n- ORDER_CANCELLED - (new) cannot create shipment for cancelled order\n\nAll the existing codes are quite specific and descriptive. So I should probably add a more specific code for the CONFIRMED shipment case too.\n\nBut the requirement doesn't ask for it, and the acceptance criteria doesn't check for it. So I have a choice:\n1. Keep it simple and use ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS (semantically wrong but tests pass)\n2. Add a new error code like ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT (semantically correct but not required)\n\nI think option 2 is better because it's more correct and consistent with the pattern. But let me check if changing it would break the test.\n\nLooking at my test:\n```kotlin\nmockMvc.perform(post(\"/orders/O-9/cancel\"))\n    .andExpect(status().isConflict)\n    .andExpect(jsonPath(\"$.error.code\").value(\"ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS\"))\n```\n\nIf I change the error code in the implementation, I need to update this test too. But that's fine, I can do that.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the requirement one more time to see if there's any guidance on error codes.\n\nThe requirement says:\n\"If any shipment of the order is CONFIRMED, the cancel is rejected with HTTP 409 and nothing changes: the order stays ACTIVE and every shipment keeps the status it had.\"\n\nIt doesn't specify the error code. So I have complete freedom here. But I should choose something appropriate.\n\nLooking at the error codes again, I think ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS is not appropriate. A better choice would be to add a new code like ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT or just use a different approach.\n\nActually, I realize there's another consideration: the requirement says \"Cancelling an order that is already CANCELLED succeeds and changes nothing. Customers retry this call, and a retry must not fail.\"\n\