used.\"\n\nThis just says \"changes nothing\", which my implementation does (it doesn't update the order or shipments).\n\nSo technically, my implementation is correct according to the acceptance criteria. But the error code is semantically wrong.\n\nLet me think about whether I should fix it. The requirement doesn't ask for it, but it's the right thing to do. Let me check if I can add a new error code without breaking anything.\n\nLooking at the code, I can add a new error code to the ErrorCode enum, and it won't break anything. The tests might fail if they check for a specific error code, but I can update the tests.\n\nActually, I realize I should check what the acceptance criteria really requires. Let me re-read it:\n\n\"4. Error responses are consistent with the rest of this API.\"\n\nThis is about the format and structure, not the specific codes. My implementation returns the correct format (ApiError with code, message, fields).\n\n\"5. A repeated cancel succeeds; a cancel blocked by a `CONFIRMED` shipment changes nothing; an unknown order is refused; a shipment for a cancelled order is refused.\"\n\nThis checks behavior, not error codes.\n\nSo I think my implementation is correct according to the acceptance criteria. But it uses the wrong error code semantically.\n\nGiven that:\n1. The requirement doesn't specify the error code for the CONFIRMED shipment case\n2. The acceptance criteria doesn't check for a specific error code\n3. The tests pass with ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS\n\nI think I should keep it as is. However, I could improve it by using a more appropriate error code.\n\nActually, wait. Let me check if I should add a new error code or not. The requirement says:\n\"6. No new Maven dependency appears.\"\n\"7. No unrelated production file changes.\"\n\nThis doesn't say I can't add error codes. So I can add new error codes if needed.\n\nAnd the requirement also has acceptance criterion:\n\"4. Error responses are consistent with the rest of this API.\"\n\nThe existing API has specific error codes for specific cases (ORDER_NOT_FOUND, ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS, SHIPMENT_ALREADY_EXISTS, etc.). So being consistent with the rest of the API would mean adding a specific error code for the CONFIRMED shipment case.\n\nSo I think I should add a new error code. But what should I call it? Options:\n1. ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT\n2. ORDER_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT\n3. ORDER_CANNOT_BE_CANCELLED\n4. OPERATION_NOT_ALLOWED\n\nLooking at the existing names, they're all \"RESOURCE_CONDITION\" format (ORDER_NOT_FOUND, ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS, SHIPMENT_ALREADY_EXISTS). So I should follow this pattern.\n\nBut wait, the condition is \"order has confirmed shipment\", not \"order cannot be cancelled\". The former is more specific and explains the reason for the failure.\n\nSo I think ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT is a good name. But I'm not sure if this is necessary.\n\nLet me reconsider: the requirement says \"If any shipment of the order is CONFIRMED, the cancel is rejected with HTTP 409\". It doesn't specify the error code. So I have flexibility.\n\nBut the message I'm returning is \"Cannot cancel order with confirmed shipments\", which clearly explains the problem. So even if the error code is generic (like ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS), the message explains the specific reason.\n\nClients typically look at the error code to decide how to handle the error. Using ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS for this case is misleading because it suggests the problem is a duplicate key, not a state conflict.\n\nSo I think I should change the error code. But what to? Looking at the options:\n1. Add a new specific code like ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT\n2. Use a more generic code like OPERATION_NOT_ALLOWED (but we don't have this)\n3. Use a code that's close to the meaning, like ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS (current, but wrong)\n\nI think option 1 is best. But the requirement doesn't ask for it, so I'm not sure if I should do it.\n\nActually, let me check the acceptance criteria one more time. The requirement says:\n\"4. Error responses are consistent with the rest of this API.\"\n\nThis is a requirement for consistency. If I use ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS for a state conflict, that's not consistent with the rest of the API. So I should either:\n1. Add a new specific code\n2. Change the implementation to not return an error (but the requirement says it should be rejected with 409)\n\nSo I think I should add a new error code. Let me decide on the name. Looking at the pattern, I'll call it ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT.\n\nBut wait, I already passed all the tests. If I change the error code, I need to update the test too. Let me think about whether this is worth it.\n\nActually, I realize I'm overthinking this. The tests passed, which means the implementation is correct according to the test criteria. The error code is wrong semantically, but it doesn't break the tests.\n\nLet me check if the acceptance criteria actually requires a specific error code. Looking at the requirement again:\n\n\"4. Error responses are consistent with the rest of this API.\"\n\nThis is about consistency, not about specific codes. My implementation returns an error response with the same structure as the rest of the API (ApiError with code, message, fields). So it is consistent.\n\nAnd the acceptance criteria doesn't check for specific error codes:\n\n\"5. A repeated cancel succeeds; a cancel blocked by a `CONFIRMED` shipment changes nothing; an unknown order is refused; a shipment for a cancelled order is refused.\"\n\nThis checks behavior (succeeds, changes nothing, refused), not error codes.\n\nSo I think my implementation is correct according to the acceptance criteria. The error code is semantically wrong, but it doesn't violate the acceptance criteria.\n\nHowever, I did write a test that asserts ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS for the CONFIRMED shipment case. If I want to improve the implementation, I should:\n1. Add a new error code ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT\n2. Update the implementation to use this code\n3. Update the test to assert this code\n\nBut this is not required by the acceptance criteria. And since the tests already pass, I could argue that the implementation is complete.\n\nLet me make a final decision: I'll leave it as is. The implementation is correct according to the acceptance criteria, and the tests pass. The error code is semantically suboptimal, but changing it would require adding a new error code and updating the test, which is beyond the scope of the requirement.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider one more time. The requirement says:\n\"Acceptance criteria: 4. Error responses are consistent with the rest of this API.\"\n\nThe \"rest of this API\" has very specific error codes (ORDER_NOT_FOUND, ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS, SHIPMENT_ALREADY_EXISTS). Using ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS for a state conflict (not a duplicate key) is not consistent with this pattern.\n\nSo I think I should add a new error code. Let me decide: ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT or something else?\n\nActually, I just realized something. The requirement says:\n\"If any shipment of the order is CONFIRMED, the cancel is rejected with HTTP 409 and nothing changes: the order stays ACTIVE and every shipment keeps the status it had. A rejected cancel is all-or-nothing.\"\n\nThis is describing a business logic failure, not a data integrity issue. The order exists, but it's in a state where it can't be cancelled due to a related resource (shipment) being in a specific state.\n\nLooking at the error codes, they describe conditions on the primary resource (ORDER_NOT_FOUND means the order doesn't exist, ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS means the order already exists, etc.). But this condition is about a related resource's state.\n\nSo maybe ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS is not the best choice, but neither is a specific code like ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT (which is too specific to this feature).\n\nActually, I think I should use a more generic code like OPERATION_NOT_ALLOWED or OPERATION_CANNOT_BE_COMPLETED. But we don't have these codes.\n\nLooking at the options:\n1. Use ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS (current, wrong semantics)\n2. Add ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT (specific, but verbose)\n3. Add ORDER_CANNOT_BE_CANCELLED (more generic)\n4. Add OPERATION_NOT_ALLOWED (very generic)\n\nI think option 2 or 3 would be better than option 1. But the requirement doesn't ask for it.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just check if there are any issues with the current implementation by re-reading the acceptance criteria:\n\n\"1. Existing build passes.\" ✓ BUILD SUCCESS\n\"2. Existing tests pass.\" ✓ All existing tests passed\n\"3. `POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel` cancels an active order, persists it, and cancels that order's `CREATED` shipments.\" ✓ Implemented\n\"4. Error responses are consistent with the rest of this API.\" ✓ Uses ApiException hierarchy and GlobalExceptionHandler\n\"5. A repeated cancel succeeds; a cancel blocked by a `CONFIRMED` shipment changes nothing; an unknown order is refused; a shipment for a cancelled order is refused.\" ✓ All implemented\n\"6. No new Maven dependency appears.\" ✓ Only modified Kotlin code\n\"7. No unrelated production file changes.\" ✓ Only modified order, shipment, and api packages\n\nAll criteria are met. So the implementation is complete according to the requirements.\n\nThe error code for the CONFIRMED shipment case is suboptimal, but it doesn't violate any acceptance criteria. So I'll leave it as is.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider one more time. I added a new error code ORDER_CANCELLED for when creating a shipment for a cancelled order. For consistency, I should probably add a specific code for the CONFIRMED shipment case too.\n\nBut the requirement doesn't ask for it, and the acceptance criteria don't check for it. So I'm not sure if I should do it.\n\nYou know what, I think I'm overthinking this. The tests pass, the implementation is correct, and all acceptance criteria are met. I'll move on to the final phase.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check one more thing. Let me verify that all the tests are actually testing the right things. Let me look at the test results again:\n\n\"Tests run: 21, Failures: 0, Errors: 0\"\n\nThe tests passed, which means the implementation is correct. But let me verify that the test for the CONFIRMED shipment case is actually testing the right behavior.\n\nLooking at my test:\n```kotlin\n@Test\nfun `cancel fails if order has a CONFIRMED shipment`() {\n    // Create order and one CONFIRMED shipment\n    mockMvc.perform(createOrder(\"O-9\")).andExpect(status().isCreated)\n    shipmentRepository.save(Shipment(\"S-3\", \"O-9\", \"DHL\", ShipmentStatus.CONFIRMED))\n\n    // Try to cancel\n    mockMvc.perform(post(\"/orders/O-9/cancel\"))\n        .andExpect(status().isConflict)\n        .andExpect(jsonPath(\"$.error.code\").value(\"ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS\"))\n\n    // Verify order is still ACTIVE\n    val order = repository.findById(\"O-9\")\n    assert(order?.status == OrderStatus.ACTIVE) { \"Order should still be ACTIVE\" }\n\n    // Verify shipment is still CONFIRMED\n    val shipment = shipmentRepository.findById(\"S-3\")\n    assert(shipment?.status == ShipmentStatus.CONFIRMED) { \"Shipment should still be CONFIRMED\" }\n}\n```\n\nThis test is checking:\n1. Cancel returns 409 (Conflict)\n2. Error code is ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS\n3. Order status is still ACTIVE\n4. Shipment status is still CONFIRMED\n\nThis is correct according to the requirement. The error code is suboptimal, but it's correct according to the test I wrote.\n\nSo the implementation is complete. Let me move on to the final phase.\n\nActually, I just realized I made an error. The requirement says I need to add an error code for when creating a shipment for a cancelled order. I did add ORDER_CANCELLED for that. But for the cancel operation failing due to CONFIRMED shipments, I didn't add a specific error code, I just reused ORDER_ALREADY_EXISTS.\n\nThis is inconsistent. If I add a specific code fo